\title{The Extended Fiat–Shamir Transform}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    Arnon and Yogev proposed a modified transform, XFS, designed so that [the diagonalisation
    attacks](dhsorens-004X) cannot be mounted against it. The idea attacks the attack's precondition
    rather than its mechanics. A self-referential statement works by arranging for the circuit and
    the verifier to query the oracle at the same points; XFS adds a proof of work and a
    prefix-avoiding padder to the challenge derivation, so that the inputs the verifier queries are
    provably outside the set a circuit inside the statement can reach. The self-reference is then
    unavailable, whatever the circuit computes.
}

\p{
    Their security claim is a claim about a model. They define an idealised model abstracting what
    the known attacks have in common — diagonalisation, in the sense that the statement talks about
    the hash — and prove unconditionally that plain Fiat–Shamir is insecure in it and XFS is secure.
    That is a stronger form of argument than a reduction to an assumption and a weaker one than a
    standard-model theorem: it says any successful attack on XFS must use something the model does
    not capture, and says nothing about what else there might be to use.
}

\p{
    Kalsta found something the model does not capture. \em{Simple Attacks Against (Extended)
    Fiat–Shamir} gives a counterexample to XFS built from a universal argument system in Barak's
    style, and — this is the part that makes it awkward — the construction is simpler than Barak's
    and does not need the heavy machinery of the earlier uninstantiability results. So the proposal
    that was to close the gap has a counterexample within a year, by a route the idealised model did
    not anticipate.
}

\p{
    XFS also costs something in the place a deployment can least afford it. The proof of work sits
    in the challenge derivation, so the verifier pays it, and the verifier's cost is the entire
    design constraint for [on-chain verification](dhsorens-003P). As described in Kalsta's account,
    XFS is defined for particular three-move proof systems over Boolean circuits, which is narrower
    than the many-round [IOP](dhsorens-002E)-based systems this corpus is about; how it would extend
    to a twenty-round [folding argument](dhsorens-003K) is not something I can currently say.
}

\p{
    Two rounds of proposal and counterexample inside a year is the honest summary of where the
    standard-model question stands, and I would not hold a deployment decision on it. The transform
    is not the variable that a system operator controls; the [adaptivity of its
    statements](dhsorens-004O) is. A system that can establish its statements are fixed before its
    hash function is chosen does not need XFS, and a system that cannot has a problem no transform
    currently on offer is proved to solve.
}

\p{
    Sources: [Towards a White-Box Secure Fiat–Shamir
    Transformation](https://eprint.iacr.org/2025/329) (Arnon, Yogev, CRYPTO 2025); Kalsta,
    \em{Simple Attacks Against (Extended) Fiat–Shamir}.
}
